{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>As long as all the days of the earth.</b> As a sign that it has a fixed end. How precious is the derash of \"surrounding, eighteen thousand\" — only we do not know one of the kinds of thousand." "<b>Seed and harvest.</b> He divided the year into two, and afterward into four. <b>Cold.</b> Corresponding to heat. <b>And summer.</b> Corresponding to winter — and they correspond to the four seasons of the year. And all of them, day and night, divide equally, for what is lacking from one is added to the other, and then the surplus returns and diminishes, and is added to what is lacking, until they are equal."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The derash reference is cryptic; possibly alluding to Ezekiel 48:35 and its rabbinic interpretation. Read hyper-literally as the text stands.", 1 "The closing phrase about day and night reaching equality is read literally; the argument concerns the balance of daylight and darkness across the seasons."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:51.801305154Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}